                    coach_r1_lowfocus:      `<strong>You've blurred structural and sequencing barriers together.</strong> Incentives and capability are structural; running 14 initiatives at once is a sequencing problem. They need different fixes — separate them or your plan will mistreat both.`,
                    coach_r2_focused:       `<strong>Focus beats breadth in execution.</strong> Four or five properly funded, owned initiatives can cross the threshold to land, where €2M-each across 14 never does. That's the courage to concentrate that the original plan lacked.`,
                    coach_r2_all:           `<strong>You've kept the full portfolio alive on €30M — about €2M each.</strong> That's the same fragmentation that stalled it: nothing crosses the threshold to land. Which 4–5 initiatives would actually move the 2027 milestones, and which do you have the courage to kill or defer?`,
                    coach_r2_nofoundations: `<strong>You've under-funded the foundations.</strong> Eco-design capability, supplier development and the incentive redesign are unglamorous and win no applause — but the visible initiatives fail without them. Starve them and you reproduce the stall.`,
                    coach_r2_flagshipheavy: `<strong>You've poured the budget into the flagship refill range.</strong> Great optics — but you have no eco-design engineers and only 2 of 38 suppliers can make mono-material packaging. It'll stick in pilot, exactly like before, and now it's the <em>visible</em> failure. What foundations needed to come first?`,
                    coach_r2_sequenced:     `<strong>That's dependency sequencing done right.</strong> A focused portfolio with foundations funded and built first means the flagship launches onto capability and suppliers that actually exist — not into the same pilot trap that stalled it six months ago.`,
                    coach_r2_flagshipfirst: `<strong>You've put €9M into the flagship in Q1 for the optics.</strong> With no eco-design engineers and 2 of 38 suppliers ready, six months on it's stuck in pilot — exactly like before, but now it's the visible failure. The unglamorous foundations had to come first.`,
                    coach_r3_incentive_right: `<strong>That's the high-leverage move.</strong> Putting real weight on sustainability in the bonus gives owners a reason to deliver, without abandoning the cost/volume focus the business still needs. It's the single hardest, most political barrier — and you took it on.`,
                    coach_r3_incentive_untouched: `<strong>Your owners have no reason to deliver.</strong> You've assigned sustainability targets to managers still bonused 100% on cost and volume. On 1 January they'll do what they're paid to do, and that isn't your strategy. Ownership without aligned incentives is hollow.`,
                    coach_r3_incentive_over: `<strong>You've over-corrected.</strong> Loading most of the bonus onto sustainability blows the cost and volume focus the business still needs to fund the transition — and hands the resisting Operations Director the argument that you'll confuse the factory. Find the balance.`,
                    coach_r3_incentive_partial: `<strong>A start, but it may be too light to move behaviour.</strong> A small sustainability weight against a dominant cost/volume bonus still tells managers where their pay really comes from. Push it into the range that genuinely shifts what they optimise.`,
                    coach_r3_sponsor:       `<strong>“Everyone's job” is no one's job.</strong> A CEO sponsor is not an accountable owner. Without a named owner per initiative whose objectives include delivery, this is the same ownerless strategy that stalled — a sponsor with no one on the hook.`,
                    coach_r3_retrain:       `<strong>Retraining on the side rarely builds real capability.</strong> Asking busy staff to become eco-design engineers in their spare time produces neither delivery nor depth. Partner to deliver now while you build the function properly.`,
                    coach_r3_nocapability:  `<strong>You've deferred the capability and assigned the targets anyway.</strong> With zero eco-design engineers and no supplier-sustainability function, the owners can't deliver what you've put on them. The capability gap is a structural barrier — build it, don't wish it away.`,
                    coach_r3_cocreate:      `<strong>That's value co-creation.</strong> Engaging the suppliers and the two retail customers as partners who help build the new model — joint roadmaps, shared investment — turns a compliance burden into the thing that makes execution feasible. They wanted a partner, not a promise.`,
                    coach_r3_compliance:    `<strong>You've sent a compliance letter and a promise.</strong> But only 2 of 38 suppliers <em>can</em> comply, and the two retailers wanted a co-creation partner, not “we'll try.” Treating the stakeholders who enable execution as targets of a mandate is exactly how this stays a deck.`,
                    coach_r4_quarterly:     `<strong>That's accountable, measurable execution.</strong> Quarterly checkpoints on the four KPIs with a barrier-removed → initiative-delivered → milestone-met chain is what turns a promise into a trajectory the board can actually track.`,
                    coach_r4_nocheckpoint:  `<strong>“We'll drive real execution this time” is what she's heard before.</strong> It's why she's reviewing you. Without quarterly execution-rate and buy-in checkpoints and an explicit barrier-to-outcome chain, this is another deck dressed as a plan.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Signalling both 2027 dates may slip is conceding the stall.</strong> The loan steps up 75 bps and the two retailers can delist — both fall due in 2027. A plan that already plans to miss them is not an execution plan.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest answer.</strong> Naming the fragile dependency, its owner, the checkpoint and the fallback — backed by quarterly tracking — is exactly the credible path from a stalled deck to delivered outcomes the director was looking for.`,
                    coach_r4_reassure:      `<strong>“It's under control” without naming the dependency won't hold.</strong> The director's whole job is to test the most fragile link. Name it, give it an owner and a checkpoint, or she'll read it as the same confidence that preceded the stall.`,
                    coach_r4_optics:        `<strong>Pointing to the flagship launch is the optics trap again.</strong> A launch event is not delivered execution. If the foundations and incentives aren't behind it, you're showing the board the very initiative most likely to fail first.`,

